 `StatusOf(role)` answers `{running, phase, doing, of, steps, wrote, saved,
 started, ended, messages[], error}` — what the GUI's status route and the
 Obsidian plugin poll.
 
+## Audit and conflicts
+
+Every change a run makes to memory is one line of
+`memory-audit.jsonl` (`LogAudit`), appended and flushed, never rewritten:
+`save`, `forget`, `remove`, `promote` and `status`, with the memory's id,
+the role, the agent, what it did (`added`, `merged`, `changed`, `forgotten`,
+`removed`, `promoted`, a new status, `refused` or `conflict`) and, when it
+didn't go through, why (`error`). A line holds no memory text — only what
+changed — so a secret in a memory never reaches the log, and one over
+`MaxAuditLine` is cut rather than written whole. A log that can't be written
+is noted with `log.Printf` and otherwise passed over: audit never stops a
+change the user asked for. Lines are appended one write at a time
+(`auditMu`), so two runs' lines are two whole lines.
+
+A machine change to what the user wrote is refused (`ConflictError`,
+`IsConflict`), and the refusal is added to `<vault>/logs/memory-conflicts.md`
+through the run's sandbox, so it is inside the folders a run may write and
+nowhere else: the memory's id, the action, the role, why it was refused
+(`origin=user`, `edited by the user`, or both) and the user's words, with
+one that looks like a secret replaced by `[redacted]`. The note is only ever
+added to (`Sandbox.Append`), and the memory itself is left exactly as it
+was — the tools check `memory.Get` before `Edit`/`Forget`/`Remove`. A change
+that leaves the user's words alone is not a conflict: a run may still save
+what is new, and forget what isn't theirs. `memory_status` is the machine's
+for a candidate it proposed; taking the user's own memory out of use is
+refused.
+
